comments.body:
OK so after some digging the ability to do pretty much anything with UART 0 has been disabled. This was done back in 2017

```c
// Attempts to use UART0 from Python has resulted in all sorts of fun errors.
    // FIXME: UART0 is disabled for now.
    if (uart_num == UART_NUM_0) {
        mp_raise_msg_varg(&mp_type_ValueError, MP_ERROR_TEXT("UART(%d) is disabled (dedicated to REPL)"), uart_num);
    }
```

mapping UART 1 to pins 1 and 3 does not work, Nor can stdin and stdout be shanghaied in a manner that would allow me to send raw byte data.. so no characters outside of the 32 to 127 range (or whatever it is), I cannot send 0x04 or 0x05. If I attempt to pass them to stdout they get converted to null characters. I have not been able to collect anything from stdin ever.. I am sending data to the ESP like gang busters but I get not one byte by using stdin.read(1)

I have seen it may time already about there being file managers available. OK so yeah. for linux. and there may be a command line based one that will run on Windows. ampy is a fine example of one, it only works about 5% of the time. I have not been able to locate a GUI based file manager that will run on Windows, so I am writing one. Because of the number of queries that Windows is going to do I have to shorten up the data that is being sent and speed it up huge amounts. Windows will query some 300+ times just to list the contents of a folder that has 20 files in it. Having it take minutes just to load the contents of a single folder that has 20 files in it is not going to work.

I need to be able to do this via the single USB connection, I need to have the baudrate  turned up as high as possible (Expressif states 5,000,000bps is the max) and I need to be able to send any character within the 0 - 255 range and not have the characters get converted into null characters.

If there is a way to do this without having to wiggle around the REPL and it capturing incoming data or anything like that, please help me out and share how to go about doing it.

The returned instance buy creating a UART using the same port number is not going to return an working instance. It is only going to reconfigure it.

You are not able to overwrite the buffers used when the UART has been created when altering UART 0. That is because of the special handling that is involved with the REPL UART.

Now it appears that there might be coding issue with respect to the ESP32 port regarding os.dupterm. the issue is that dupterm doesn't allow for stepping in between and stopping input and output from the REPL. It only appears to do what the function name suggests and it duplicates the input and output. This is able to be seen in this code block that is located in the mphapport.c file in the esp32 port.

```c

mp_uint_t mp_hal_stdout_tx_strn(const char *str, size_t len) {
    // Only release the GIL if many characters are being sent
    mp_uint_t ret = len;
    bool did_write = false;
    bool release_gil = len > 20;
    if (release_gil) {
        MP_THREAD_GIL_EXIT();
    }
    #if CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG
    usb_serial_jtag_tx_strn(str, len);
    did_write = true;
    #elif CONFIG_USB_OTG_SUPPORTED
    usb_tx_strn(str, len);
    did_write = true;
    #endif
    #if MICROPY_HW_ENABLE_UART_REPL
    uart_stdout_tx_strn(str, len);
    did_write = true;
    #endif
    if (release_gil) {
        MP_THREAD_GIL_ENTER();
    }
    int dupterm_res = mp_os_dupterm_tx_strn(str, len);
    if (dupterm_res >= 0) {
        did_write = true;
        ret = MIN((mp_uint_t)dupterm_res, ret);
    }
    return did_write ? ret : 0;
}

```

The repl uses this function to send data out over the UART. You can see where it calls uart_stdout_tx_strn and then it calls mp_os_dupterm_tx_strn. it doesn't use the returned value from mp_os_dupterm_tx_strn to decide if it should call uart_stdout_tx_strn. 

That function should be changed so the value returned when calling mp_os_dupterm_tx_strn would be the data that is wanting to be sent to the uart and not the number of bytes that have be written.

It looks like there is no way to have both the REPL and a device on UART 0 without modifying the micropython code to allow it to happen. from what I have been able to see is that you are only able to send information into the repl and view data coming out of the repl but you are not able to stop the repl from communicating with the UART nor are you able to send data to the UART using os.dupterm


https://docs.micropython.org/en/latest/library/os.html#os.dupterm

The header for this section is an incorrect description of what os.dupterm does.

Instead of reading "Terminal redirection and duplication" it should read "REPL duplication". because you are not able to change the behavior of the terminal at all and you are not able to redirect anything. the only thing that is able to be done is a duplication of the input and output from the REPL over another form of communications. to redirect something means to move from one to another. This is not able to be done.
